\begin_layout Section
Comments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observe that the 
\family typewriter
Student.java
\family default
 file in the example that accompanies this supplement contains no comments.
 Too often, the writing of comments distracts from the pursuit of correctness.
 Comments are, by definition, the parts of the program that are ignored
 by the compiler.
 Quite sensibly, programmers follow the compiler's lead in ignoring comments,
 which are therefore often stale or incorrect as soon as they are written.
 Novice programmers tend to be prolix and uninformative in their comments.
 For example, the comment 
\family typewriter
Iterate
\family default
 just before a loop, the like of which I have seen many times in student
 code, is both redundant and entirely uninformative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tests that you have just written are superior to comments as documentation
 because they are 
\emph on
machine-checkable
\emph default
.
 If a test fails, it is the programmer's responsibility to review the test
 and decide whether it is the test itself which is broken or the object
 code.
 In either case, the programmer will take action, either to correct the
 test or the object code.
 Comments do not have such a self-regulating mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At some time during your career you are likely to find yourself in a situation
 where you are required to write comments.
 In that case, you should adhere to the comment writing guidelines in place
 where you work.
 The time you spend in college is too precious to waste on learning this
 tedious activity.
 On the other hand, if, by some chance, you already have the habit of writing
 useful comments do not desist now.
 Your adjustment to a workplace where commenting is enforced will be that
 much easier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Formatting Your Code Correctly
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Well formatted code is easier to read and consequently to understand.
 Understanding is a prerequisite to correctness; therefore good formatting
 aids correctness.
 Checkstyle
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "checkstyle"

\end_inset

 is a tool that identifies instances where a programmer has failed to adhere
 to a coding standard.
 Checkstyle is configurable to different coding standards.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Running Checkstyle in BlueJ
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Checkstyle is available as a BlueJ extension and must be added to BlueJ
 before it can be used.
 Once it is available in BlueJ a 
\family typewriter
Checkstyle
\family default
 choice will be available in the 
\family typewriter
Tools
\family default
 menu.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Open the 
\emph on
lab-classes
\emph default
 project.
 Select 
\family typewriter
Checkstyle
\family default
 and you will see a new window that identifies all the formatting errors
 that Checkstyle has located in the project.
 You will see that the Checkstyle extension uses a particular default configurat
ion file that places a good deal of emphasis on comments.
 Instead of using the default configuration, change your configuration so
 that it refers to the file 
\family typewriter
default_checks.xml
\family default
