\begin_layout Subsection
final
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Your Checkstyle configuration requires the use of the keyword 
\family typewriter
final
\family default
 for every local variable that is unmodified.
 In Java, all formal parameters are local variables.
 Notice that no formal parameter in any method in the 
\family typewriter
Student
\family default
 class is modified so all are declared 
\family typewriter
final
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using the 
\family typewriter
final
\family default
 modifier you communicate to all future readers of your code, and to the
 Java compiler, that the modified variable is a name for a value and is
 not to be assigned to.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the instance variable 
\family typewriter
id
\family default
 is also declared final.
 It is set once in the constructor and then may not be changed subsequently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In general your code will be easier to understand if it contains fewer assignmen
ts; all other things being equal, the frequent occurrence of the 
\family typewriter
final
\family default
 keyword is a good sign.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
this
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every method in the 
\family typewriter
Student
\family default
 class takes an implicit parameter, 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
.
 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
 is a reference to the particular object on which the method has been invoked.
 All methods that work in this way are called instance methods.
 Other sorts of method will be introduced when the need arises.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whenever any method uses an instance variable of the class we specify that
 instance variable by the 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
 keyword, followed by a period, followed by the name of the instance field.
 For instance, in the 
\family typewriter
getStudentID()
\family default
 method the instance field 
\family typewriter
id
\family default
 is specified by 
\family typewriter
this.id
\family default
.
 Your Checkstyle configuration requires that all instance fields be specified
 using 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
.
 Within the tiny methods of the 
\family typewriter
Student
\family default
 class the use of 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
 is almost redundant since there are no locally declared variables other
 than final parameters; in larger methods, however, the use of 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
 serves to distinguish the instance variables from locally declared variables
 where they are used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section you have learned how to construct simple unit tests for
 the methods of a class.
 Unit tests have a value that comments lack.
 Format your code consistently so that you, and others, can understand it
 more easily.
 Use 
\family typewriter
final
\family default
 in every case where your variables are intended to be immutable.
 Use 
\family typewriter
this
\family default
 whenever you are referring to an instance variable in order to visually
 distinguish instance variables from locally declared variables.
\end_layout

