 can not evaluate the expression and Code Pad does not report a type.
 Drag your 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
 object icon from the Code Pad onto the Object Bench.
 Observe that the Object Inspector icon also indicates the type of the object,
 i.e., 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Using Assertions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Exceptions guard against misuse of an object by others.
 Since the 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
 constructor throws an exception whenever the value of the 
\family typewriter
ticketPrice
\family default
 argument is less than 0 all classes that use a 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
 object must obey the 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
's contract by making sure to call its constructor with non-negative arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The arguments of the constructor are used to specify its 
\family typewriter
ticketPrice
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
total
\family default
 instance variables.
 Neither of these fields should ever become less than 0.
 We can prevent external code from setting these values incorrectly by means
 of exceptions.
 However, we also wish to prevent internal code, i.e., methods in 
\family typewriter
TicketMachine
\family default
 from setting the values of these instance fields incorrectly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any fact that must hold for an object throughout its existence is called
 a class invariant.
 Our design requires that the values of 
\family typewriter
ticketPrice
\family default
, 
\family typewriter
total
\family default
, and 
\family typewriter
balance
\family default
 always be non-negative.
 Thus, our class invariant is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
this.ticketPrice\geq0\wedge this.total\geq0\wedge{\textstyle this.balance}\geq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use assertions to detect incorrect values of instance fields.
 Every time a method is called the class invariant is checked by means of
 an assertion.
 If the expression evaluates to false, an 
\family typewriter
AssertionError
\family default
, a special type of exception, is thrown.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of assertions is not to detect the errors of other in using
 our code.
 Their purpose is to keep the programmer honest and to give early warning
 of an incorrect assumption in the design.
 During development, assertions should be enabled so that errors are detected.
 When code is deployed assertions are generally disabled.
 However, if a bug is reported an investigating programmer can re-enable
 assertions and use them to detect if some underlying assumption in the
 code has been violated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that assertions, like unit tests and unlike comments, are 
\emph on
machine-checkable
\emph default
 documentation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Exercise:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Try out some assertions in the Code Pad.
 Some suggestions are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
assert(true);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
assert(false);
\end_layout

