\begin_layout Section
Clock
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Overview
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section we present an alternative approach to the design of the
 clock in the textbook.
 The key change in our design is that we separate the data from the display
 of the data.
 We distinguish the clock's computational part, i.e., the recording and measuring
 of time from its display part.
 In the example in the BlueJ textbook, the display of the clock determined
 how time was computed.
 Since the clock displayed hours and minutes, two 
\family typewriter
NumberDisplay
\family default
 objects were used to store the clock's hours and minutes respectively.
 We can change our perspective by realizing that hours are just a convenient
 grouping for the minutes, just like dozens are a convenient grouping for
 eggs.
 This means that we can make a clock that simply records the number of minutes
 that have elapsed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The clock package
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the computational part we make use of two classes, 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
.
 These classes are grouped in the 
\family typewriter
clock
\family default
 package.
 A package is a higher level of organization that the Java language provides.
 We choose to group the two classes together because the 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 class relies entirely on the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class and because we do not want any users of our 
\family typewriter
clock
\family default
 package to make use of the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class independently, i.e., not by making a 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observe the 
\family typewriter
package
\family default
 declaration at the top of both files.
 This declaration is necessary so that the compiler will recognize that
 the classes belong to the 
\family typewriter
clock
\family default
 package.
 Moreover, note that the files are contained in a directory called 
\family typewriter
clock
\family default
.
 Java requires that the directory in which the files are contained has the
 same name as the package to which the files belong.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observe also that the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class declaration is not public.
 This means that only classes that belong to the package can make use of
 it.
 The 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 class is public, so all external users of the package can use this class.
\end_layout

