\begin_layout Subsection
The Clock class
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 object uses a single 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 object which counts the minutes that have elapsed since the start of the
 clock's day.
 After the total number of minutes in a single day have elapsed, the counter
 automatically starts over again from 0.
 The Clock object interprets the counter's minutes in terms of hours and
 minutes in the day.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implementation of 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 is complete and a reasonably complete test class has also been constructed.
 Some of the tests pass; these are test methods of the 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 class that do not depend on methods of the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class.
 Some of the test fail; these are test methods of the 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 class that depend on methods of the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that the 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 constructor takes two parameters: the number of hours in a cycle and the
 number of minutes in an hour.
 Methods of this class make use of the instance variables 
\family typewriter
hoursPerCycle
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
minutesPerHour
\family default
 wherever appropriate.
 Observe how the constructor makes use of them in calculating the number
 of minutes in a cycle to pass as an argument to the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 constructor.
 By parameterizing the clock in this way it can be made much more general.
 Choose the hours per cycle to be 12 and the minutes per hour to be 60 and
 it is a civilian time clock.
 Change the hours per cycle to be 24 and this clock calculates military
 time.
 Change the hours to 180 and perhaps this clock computes the time on an
 alien planet that takes much longer to complete a single rotation than
 does our earth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observe that all instance variables of the 
\family typewriter
Clock
\family default
 class have been declared final; only some of the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
's instance variables can change.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Counter
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implementation of 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 is incomplete; your task is to complete it.
 We observe that the Counter class has an invariant:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
this.limit>0\wedge0\leq this.value<this.limit
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remember that the 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 class is package private; only code within the package can call the constructor.
 Therefore, we should use an assertion, rather than exceptions, to detect
 bad argument values.
 The assertion condition is just like the class invariant except that it
 is a statement about the values of the parameters, not the instance variables.
 If the parameters are invalid then we should not construct the object,
 so a condition about the instance variables is pointless.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
limit>0\wedge0\leq value<limit
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

