\begin_layout Section*
Specifying the Counter class
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Post-conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we write a unit test for a method we have in our mind some notion of
 the specification of the method.
 For example, we could state the specification of the method 
\family typewriter
Counter.getValue()
\family default
 in English in the following manner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
The 
\family typewriter
getValue()
\family default
 method returns the value of the 
\family typewriter
value
\family default
 instance variable of its 
\family typewriter
Counter
\family default
 object.
 It does not update 
\family typewriter
value
\family default
 or 
\family typewriter
limit
\family default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following is a propositional formula with the same meaning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{center}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{math}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
mbox{@return} = 
\backslash
mbox{this.value} 
\backslash
wedge 
\backslash
mbox{this.value} = 
\backslash
mbox{this.value}_{prev} 
\backslash
wedge 
\backslash
mbox{this.limit} = 
\backslash
mbox{this.limit}_{prev}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{math}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{center}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
@return
\emph default
 is used to describe the value that the method returns, and the subscript
 
\emph on
prev
\emph default
